ue you may  actually lose some of the low or high  boiler components they're not  transferred  effectively from the inlet to the column  and thus we will skew the  quantification of the sample so there  are a number of tests that we have to  meet to demonstrate uh reduction of  boiling point discrimination and i'll  show how we meet those  okay so  uh tph uh using ultra-fast gas  chromatography and i've abbreviated that  by ufgc  uh  is shown here so  the fact that we are not doing compound  speciation or trying to separate all the  compounds  is  an ideal situation for trying to apply a  method to ultra fast gc because we don't  really have to worry about  chromatographic resolution  so with ultra fast gc the definition of  that is typically its analysis times in  the one to three minute range and the  way that we achieve this is with rapid  column heating so typically we'll ramp  the oven temperature for the gc column  at 100 degrees per minute or higher over  the entire temperature program range so  for  this particular method it'll be from 40  to 350 degrees  another technique for ultra fast dc is  employing short  narrow columns  typically in the three to five meter  range whereas most of us who use gc  columns will be using them somewhere  between say 15 and 30 or 60 meters  this is quite a short column and shorter  columns of course produce faster  analysis time but it does that at the  expense of chromatographic resolution  but as i said for this method we're not  seeking chromatographic resolution we  just need to get that that profile or  that envelope of hydrocarbons  through the column as quickly as  possible  then finally the third technique that's  defines ultrafast gc  are using higher carrier flow rates uh  typically we'll go greater than five  mils a minute for those most narrow bore  columns we're usually running column  flow rates from about half a millimeter  to  maybe two or three mills a minute so we  go quite a bit faster when using ultra  fast gc techniques  some of the challenges that are uh  presented to us when trying to do ultra  fast gc is first of all retention time  precision  and  what we're trying to do here is make  sure that the retention times don't  drift  and  when we're ramping the oven very quickly  and using  high constant flow rates there's an  interaction between the  column ramping temperature  and the control of the pressure and  that's flow through the column that has  to be consistent from run to run  otherwise you won't get good retention  time precision if you don't have good  retention time precision  then trying to  integrate the  envelope of the compounds that are  eluding and identify the marker peaks  can become very difficult  so with ultrafast gc you need a  good interaction between very precise  column temperature  control and very precise flow control  while you're rapidly heating that column  another challenge for ultra fast gc  particularly for this method is column  lifetime  since we're going up to 350 degrees uh  that rapid heating and that higher  temperature that's being applied to the  column  can cause the columns of the grade  there's several different modes of  degradation that can happen  first  if you're using  columns that have a direct heating  element  wound with them  one of the examples of this is  there are low thermal mass columns  rapid  column heating and then cooling  to high temperatures can affect some of  the heating elements there and cause  failure one of the primary modes of  failure is the polyamide coating on the  column  these columns are glass but they have a  polymer coating that makes them flexible  so that when they're bent the glass  doesn't break this polyamide coating  though is not totally in there to the  atmosphere and it can break down so when  we're rapidly heating the high  temperatures the polyamide coating will  eventually oxidize over time and as it  oxidizes it will expose the glass and  that can cause column breakage  th