ue you may  actually lose some of the low or high  boiler components they're not  transferred  effectively from the inlet to the column  and thus we will skew the  quantification of the sample so there  are a number of tests that we have to  meet to demonstrate uh reduction of  boiling point discrimination and i'll  show how we meet those  okay so  uh tph uh using ultra-fast gas  chromatography and i've abbreviated that  by ufgc  uh  is shown here so  the fact that we are not doing compound  speciation or trying to separate all the  compounds  is  an ideal situation for trying to apply a  method to ultra fast gc because we don't  really have to worry about  chromatographic resolution  so with ultra fast gc the definition of  that is typically its analysis times in  the one to three minute range and the  way that we achieve this is with rapid  column heating so typically we'll ramp  the oven temperature for the gc column  at 100 degrees per minute or higher over  the entire temperature program range so  for  this particular method it'll be from 40  to 350 degrees  another technique for ultra fast dc is  employing short  narrow columns  typically in the three to five meter  range whereas most of us who use gc  columns will be using them somewhere  between say 15 and 30 or 60 meters  this is quite a short column and shorter  columns of course produce faster  analysis time but it does that at the  expense of chromatographic resolution  but as i said for this method we're not  seeking chromatographic resolution we  just need to get that that profile or  that envelope of hydrocarbons  through the column as quickly as  possible  then finally the third technique that's  defines ultrafast gc  are using higher carrier flow rates uh  typically we'll go greater than five  mils a minute for those most narrow bore  columns we're usually running column  flow rates from about half a millimeter  to  maybe two or three mills a minute so we  go quite a bit faster when using ultra  fas