ent and then all the  components from the inlet and give us a  little bit of solid focusing and then  we're going to rapidly ramp up uh at 250  degrees a minute the maximum ramp rate  for intubo to 350 degrees a minute to  very quickly get that hydrocarbon  profile  through the column and into the detector  and that will hold for half a minute and  finally the fid temperature is held at  350.  so let's take a look at some data now so  the first thing we did when we were  testing this method within tuvo is we  wanted to make sure that the um  the column performance was good over a  long period of time  because we're doing really ultra fast gc  this will allow laboratories to run a  lot more samples in a given workday  and so we want to make sure that with  that large number of samples that are  being run  the conditions or the performance of the  system isn't changing in a short period  of time with the large number of samples  that are run  so this is a performance test mix  for the ultra fast gc  tph method here we see our c10 to c40  peaks with some other peaks in between  here  you can see now the runtime that c40 is  eluding in less than two minutes  it looks like the the c10 elutes just  under one minute  and what we're looking at here is a  agreement between the first injection  and injection number fifteen hundred and  you can see that the retention times  really haven't shifted very much they've  gotten a little bit shorter but not  significantly shorter also you can see  the peak shape is maintained so  although we're losing some stationary  phase because the phase will bleed off  over time  we're not really losing so much that  it's affecting the peak shape we're just  getting a little bit shorter retention  time  and  for various systems that we've tested  we've gone up to 3000 injections before  the column needed to be changed  so let's look at the inlet  discrimination test this is an important  test you have to meet the  16703 requirements before you can really  run any samples  so you can purchase a  a performance test mix for  discrimination this contains  hydrocarbons in a heptane solvent from  c10 to c40 and we're going every two  column or carbon numbers so c10 c12 etc  all the way up to c40 now according to  the method  you look at your c20 peak and that  becomes your reference so that has a  defined recovery of  one  and then you look at the c40 peak and  you ratio the area between the c40 and  the c20  and  that ratio has to be greater than 0.8  and you can see here for this method  or for this  run we're getting 0.93 so this is  essentially telling us that we're not  losing any of the higher boiling or  lower blowing components  during the injection period because you  can see here the c10 recovery is 0.98 we  pretty much stay almost 1 all the way up  to c36  c38  and then c40 is 0.93  which is well within the  range specified by the method  i also want to take a look at  the calibration  performance so the method requires you  to run the middle standard so peak num  standard number three you run it three  times and then you look at the total  area of the  aleutian profile of the hydrocarbons so  here's our c-10 peak and our c-40 peak  we set our integrator to sum all the  area at the end of the aleutian of the  c10 and the beginning evolution of the  c40 and we just look at that total area  under this profile  and we do that three times we get the  average  and then the standard deviation and the  relative standard deviation  and according to the method the rsd has  to be less than five percent rsd  you can see here we're less than two  percent rsd  so this is telling us that we've met the  discrimination test we're meeting the  calibration test  and then finally you have to look at the  calibration curve itself  and you need to have at least a 0.99  correlation coefficient and here we have  a six level calibration  and our correlation coefficient is  essentially almost five four nines  so we're meeting that test as well  okay so let's look at a real sample  that's being run here so as i said i  took uh the two samples and extracted  them  multiple times and then  we'll do multiple injections of those  samples but let's take a look at one of  them so this is the soil sample so this  is our bam uo21 this is a  soil sample that's been certified to  have a mineral oil contamination in it  so this is the actual run so here's our  heptane solvent front  we can easily separate our c10 marker  from the solvent front  and then our final marker or c that  should be c40 sorry  that's a typo so uh  we integrate between the c10 and the c40  and we also want to make sure that our  retention times are the same  so let's take a look at another extract  that i did of the same material and  overlay that now you can see that this  chromatogram is if blue  and when i do the overlay i'll color  that chromatogram in  red  and as we all know if we mix red and  blue we get purple so let's take a look  at  how good the overlap is between our two  sample preps run on a tubo so we'll move  that on here  and you can see that we get near perfect  overlay of the two samples two  separately prepared samples  run on  two different runs you can see now the  chromatogram looks purple so it gives us  a idea that there's a near perfect  overlay both in retention time  and in peak  intensity so we're getting exceptionally  good  precision on our retention times  and also we're not getting any inlet  discrimination or removal of the higher  boiling components  let's take a look at the same sample  this is 10 different sample preps that  were run  and we're looking now overall the  retention time stability  and you can see here i focus on  the  c10 peak which is our first marker and  the c40 peak which is our second marker  so i zoomed in on those and you can see  the range here of the retention times so  for the c10 is 0.751 to 0.754  so that's ex extremely precise retention  time precision and again for the higher  boiling components it's even better  we're only .001 minutes difference  between the high and the lows of these  10 overlaps so we get  rsds for retention time precision that  are much less than one for the c10 and  much less than 0.1 for the c40  okay so these are two  of the samples that we ran so just  showing example chromatograms so in the  upper one we've we've seen this before  this is the uh the mineral oil sample in  the  soil  that's extracted from the soil and this  is the mineral oil contamination in the  river sediment river sediment is quite a  bit more challenging because as you can  imagine there's  more of a  natural  organic load in there so  when you extract this and clean it up it  looks like a much dirtier sample and  that's very important to use the the  solid phase extraction cleanup at the  end to eliminate all that  then finally let's take a look at the  quantitative results so as i said  i prepared each one of the samples  twice ran them  five times each for a total of 10 runs  so this is the soil sample this is the  river sediment sample  and so we can see uh what intubal is  going to deliver is method accuracy  so look at the mean here for our  two samples that i prepared so i have 3  500 milligrams per kilogram on sample on  the sample prep 1  and 3484  for sample prep 2 of the soil sample and  the certified value the consensus value  that bam has published with the method  is 3560 plus or minus 260. so you can  see that we have near perfect agreement  within 2-0  running the ultra-fast method  uh compared to laboratories running the  standard iso 16703 method which is about  a 30 or 35-minute run time  again if we look at  excuse me the river sediment samples  these are a little more challenging of  course because they have a higher matrix  load a lot more cleanup that has to be  done  you can see again our two sample preps  and our five runs of each one of those  and we have uh a measured value of eight  seven zero nine milligrams per kilogram  and eight six four seven  and again you can see that is near  perfect agreement with the certified  value of 8270 plus or minus 550.  so we're getting very good accuracy  we're really agreeing with those  laboratories that are running the  standard slower  iso method for tph  we also look at the instrument precision  so if we just look at the rsds for these  results you see that the rsds for all of  them are less than one percent  and so this shows very high instrument  precision but we also want to look at  the method precision so the method  precision  includes not only the instrumental  analysis but also the sample prep  so  the way the instrument or the method  precision is defined as reproducibility  so  small r  and the way that you do this is you  measure the difference between two  identical analyses of the same sample  and that difference has to be less than  what's published in the method  so for the soil sample the difference  between two sequential analyses has to  be less than 136 milligrams per kilogram  if you see here our difference on  average is about 60  uh milligrams per  kilogram  so this again showing very high method  precision for the soil sample the  difference between two  identical analyses  of the same sample  is should be less than 337 milligrams  per kilogram and we're measuring 126. so  not only are we getting good instrument  precision but the method precision is  also extremely high  so in summary  we're able to develop an ultra fast  gc method  that completes a total petroleum  hydrocarbon sample run in three minutes  as opposed to 30 or 35 minutes and we do  that by using shorter columns and take  advantage of the intubo fast oven  programming  with the sixth generation epc to deliver  precise hydrogen carrier constant flow  rates  the  intubogc has its direct column heating  and the sixth generation epc so that's  what makes this possible  and because of this there's no need to  do any post-run  lineup manipulation of the data  some  [Music]  instrument vendors will do that they're  not getting good retention time  precision so they'll use  chemometric techniques to then adjust  that retention time there's no need to  do that we naturally and organically get  extremely good retention time precision  with our direct column heating and our  pneumatics  column of column flow control  so this ultra fast gc method it meets  all of the iso performance benchmarks  for selectivity resolution  discrimination and calibration  so we've shown that the quality  quantitative results for the certified  reference materials  show extremely good agreement with other  laboratories running the slower  version of the iso method  and then we also have  extremely good repeatability compared to  what we'll typically find with the  published iso 16 703 method  so for the value proposition for what  intubo can bring to your laboratory  using ultrafast gc is lower cost of  ownership and return on investment you  can improve your laboratory  throughput  up to a factor of 10 or even higher  we get greater column lifetime  the guard chip will replace a retention  gap and protect the column from  matrix contamination and then also  it's a simpler column format and there's  less degradation  less complexity to the column when doing  this rapid heating over and over again  to high temperatures so the columns  should last longer  using an ultra fast gc method within  tuvo  ease of use  because of the high throughput of this  laboratories are typically used to  changing the columns fairly often the  columns will degrade over time mostly  related to the stationary phase  degradation  and within tuvo i didn't really go into  it but some of our other webinar series  will  it's easier and faster to change the  columns especially compared to something  like ltm  or our low thermal mass columns there's  no complex unions or connections to make  and the guard chip is easy to change and  that eliminates retention gap  trimming so for further information for  this particular application  we do have an application note that's  published this is the document number so  if you go to agilent's website type this  document number into the search field  you'll get a pdf of the  the application there  so that concludes today's webinar on  showing how intubo  can  be a financial and a  technological  improvement to your laboratory  specifically using its capability for  ultra-fast gc  and i'd like to turn it back over to our  audience to see if there's any  questions all righty wonderful thank you  so much jim for that wonderful  presentation  so before we go to our q a session i  just want to open up our short polling  survey  concerning today's presentation  while this poll is optional we do  appreciate you taking the time to answer  each question because it helps us with  modifications  for our future into a webinar  that poll is now open on the lower right  side of your console we thank you for  completing this survey  all right and on to our q a session  concerning today's presentation you may  ask questions at any time by placing  them in the question box located on your  webex platform  if you would like to ask a question over  the phone line please click the raise  hand icon located on the bottom right of  your webex  i will receive this notification and  unmute your line  where you will let me ask you a question  alrighty so our first question  so we have noticed with the fast rapid  heating and cooling of our intubo gcs  the temperature sensors for the guard  chip and detector seem to fault after a  couple of months of use  is there any development on improving  the sensors to handle the higher  temperatures  yeah i know that with the first  generation  of ventuvo  particularly if we're running high  temperatures there there have been some  decoupling of the heater element from  the guard chip heater  those have been redesigned and there was  a service note that was put out and so  those can be upgraded  and  the other thing you want to make sure  is that you  don't heat the guard chip more than  necessary  in this case i was using for this  analysis the  oven track mode so it stayed at its high  temperature for a relatively short  period of time  and that worked perfectly well for this  analysis  some customers uh  think that or will suspect that  hotter is better and that's not always  necessarily the case  so um  you want to make sure that  you don't heat it  first to too high of a temperature for  too long over a period of time also when  the instrument is idle you probably want  to have a idle mode or an idle method  that has that at a lower temperature and  leaving it at say 150 degrees is  perfectly fine  that will  avoid buildup of any contamination say  from your carrier gas or  from any septum  bleed that may  work its way through  wonderful thank you  uh next question  why did you choose helium as the carrier  gas  why not h2 or n2 considering helium  stored it  shortages and most tph methods are ran  by environmental labs  which are all about cost saving measures  right so that's uh  when  when we did this application in 2016  there wasn't as  the supply and the cost of helium had  come down from  previous problems that had happened  several years before that and in the  subsequent time uh  the  shortage and expense of helium has  gotten  worse again so that's something that we  definitely want to go back and take a  look at now based on other experience  with converting methods from helium to  hydrogen this would be actually a fairly  easy conversion um  what we could do is uh just leave the um  the hold up time or the linear velocity  the same as the helium and in that way  we won't get any difference in retention  time so it'll all be roughly the same  just with the hydrogen carrier gas  there may be actually  a way to do a little bit of speed  improvement again with hydrogen  nitrogen  i didn't really think about